.Sh DESCRIPTION
OpenSSL memory allocation is handled by the
.Sy OPENSSL_xxx
API. These are generally macro's that add the standard C
.Sy __FILE__
and
.Sy __LINE__
parameters and call a lower-level
.Sy CRYPTO_xxx
API. Some functions do not add those parameters, but exist for
consistency.
.Pp
.Fn OPENSSL_malloc_init
does nothing and does not need to be called.
It is included for compatibility with older versions of OpenSSL.
.Pp
.Fn OPENSSL_malloc ,
.Fn OPENSSL_realloc ,
and
.Fn OPENSSL_free
are like the C
.Xr malloc 3 ,
.Xr realloc 3 ,
and
.Xr free 3
functions.
.Fn OPENSSL_zalloc
calls
.Xr memset 3
to zero the memory before returning.
.Pp
.Fn OPENSSL_aligned_alloc
operates just as OPENSSL_malloc does, but it allows for the caller to
specify an alignment value, for instances in which the default alignment
of malloc is insufficient for the callers needs.
Note, the alignment value must be a power of 2, and the size specified
must be a multiple of the alignment.
NOTE: The call to
.Fn OPENSSL_aligned_alloc
accepts a 3rd argument,
.Fa freeptr
which must point to a void pointer.
On some platforms, there is no available library call to obtain memory
allocations greater than what malloc provides.
In this case, OPENSSL_aligned_alloc implements its own alignment
routine, allocating additional memory and offsetting the returned
pointer to be on the requested alignment boundary.
In order to safely free allocations made by this method, the caller must
return the value in the
.Fa freeptr
variable, rather than the returned pointer.
.Pp
.Fn OPENSSL_clear_realloc
and
.Fn OPENSSL_clear_free
should be used when the buffer at
.Fa addr
holds sensitive information.
The old buffer is filled with zero's by calling
.Fn OPENSSL_cleanse
before ultimately calling
.Fn OPENSSL_free .
If the argument to
.Fn OPENSSL_free
is NULL, nothing is done.
.Pp
.Fn OPENSSL_cleanse
fills
.Fa ptr
of size
.Fa len
with a string of 0's.
Use
.Fn OPENSSL_cleanse
with care if the memory is a mapping of a file.
If the storage controller uses write compression, then it's possible
that sensitive tail bytes will survive zeroization because the block of
zeros will be compressed.
If the storage controller uses wear leveling, then the old sensitive
data will not be overwritten; rather, a block of 0's will be written at
a new physical location.
.Pp
.Fn OPENSSL_strdup ,
.Fn OPENSSL_strndup
and
.Fn OPENSSL_memdup
are like the equivalent C functions, except that memory is allocated by
calling the
.Fn OPENSSL_malloc
and should be released by calling
.Fn OPENSSL_free .
.Pp
.Fn OPENSSL_strlcpy ,
.Fn OPENSSL_strlcat
and
.Xr OPENSSL_strnlen 3
are equivalents of the common C library functions and are provided for
portability.
.Pp
.Fn OPENSSL_strtoul
is a wrapper around the POSIX function strtoul, with the same behaviors
listed in the POSIX documentation, with the additional behavior that it
validates the input
.Fa str
and
.Fa num
parameters for not being NULL, and confirms that at least a single byte
of input has been consumed in the translation, returning an error in the
event that no bytes were consumed.
.Pp
If no allocations have been done, it is possible to "swap out" the
default implementations for
.Fn OPENSSL_malloc ,
.Fn OPENSSL_realloc
and
.Fn OPENSSL_free
and replace them with alternate versions.
.Xr CRYPTO_get_mem_functions 3
function fills in the given arguments with the function pointers for the
current implementations.
With
.Xr CRYPTO_set_mem_functions 3 ,
you can specify a different set of functions.
If any of
.Sy malloc_fn ,
.Sy realloc_fn ,
or
.Sy free_fn
are NULL, then the function is not changed.
While it's permitted to swap out only a few and not all the functions
with
.Xr CRYPTO_set_mem_functions 3 ,
it's recommended to swap them all out at once.
.Pp
If the library is built with the
.Qo Li crypto-mdebug Qc
option, then one function,
.Fn CRYPTO_get_alloc_counts ,
and two additional environment variables,
.Sy OPENSSL_MALLOC_FAILURES
and
.Sy OPENSSL_MALLOC_FD ,
are available.
.Pp
The function
.Fn CRYPTO_get_alloc_counts
fills in the number of times each of
.Fn CRYPTO_malloc ,
.Fn CRYPTO_realloc ,
and
.Fn CRYPTO_free
have been called, into the values pointed to by
.Fa mcount ,
.Fa rcount ,
and
.Fa fcount ,
respectively.
If a pointer is NULL, then the corresponding count is not stored.
.Pp
The variable
.Sy OPENSSL_MALLOC_FAILURES
controls how often allocations should fail.
It is a set of fields separated by semicolons, which each field is a
count (defaulting to zero) and an optional atsign and percentage
(defaulting to 100). If the count is zero, then it lasts forever.
For example,
.Qo Li 100;@25 Qc
or
.Qo Li 100@0;0@25 Qc
means the first 100 allocations pass, then all other allocations (until
the program exits or crashes) have a 25% chance of failing.
The length of the value of
.Sy OPENSSL_MALLOC_FAILURES
must be 256 or fewer characters.
.Pp
If the variable
.Sy OPENSSL_MALLOC_FD
is parsed as a positive integer, then it is taken as an open file
descriptor.
This is used in conjunction with
.Sy OPENSSL_MALLOC_FAILURES
described above.
For every allocation it will log details about how many allocations
there have been so far, what percentage chance there is for this
allocation failing, and whether it has actually failed.
The following example in classic shell syntax shows how to use this
(will not work on all platforms):
.Bd -literal
  OPENSSL_MALLOC_FAILURES='200;@10'
  export OPENSSL_MALLOC_FAILURES
  OPENSSL_MALLOC_FD=3
  export OPENSSL_MALLOC_FD
  ...app invocation... 3>/tmp/log$$
.Ed
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn OPENSSL_malloc_init ,
.Fn OPENSSL_free ,
.Fn OPENSSL_clear_free
.Fn CRYPTO_free ,
.Fn CRYPTO_clear_free
and
.Xr CRYPTO_get_mem_functions 3
return no value.
.Pp
.Fn OPENSSL_malloc ,
.Fn OPENSSL_aligned_alloc ,
.Fn OPENSSL_zalloc ,
.Fn OPENSSL_realloc ,
.Fn OPENSSL_clear_realloc ,
.Fn CRYPTO_malloc ,
.Fn CRYPTO_zalloc ,
.Fn CRYPTO_realloc ,
.Fn CRYPTO_clear_realloc ,
.Fn OPENSSL_strdup ,
and
.Fn OPENSSL_strndup
return a pointer to allocated memory or NULL on error.
.Pp
.Xr CRYPTO_set_mem_functions 3
returns 1 on success or 0 on failure (almost always because allocations
have already happened).
.Pp
.Fn CRYPTO_mem_leaks ,
.Fn CRYPTO_mem_leaks_fp ,
.Fn CRYPTO_mem_leaks_cb ,
.Fn CRYPTO_set_mem_debug ,
and
.Fn CRYPTO_mem_ctrl
are deprecated and are no-ops that always return -1.
.Fn OPENSSL_mem_debug_push ,
.Fn OPENSSL_mem_debug_pop ,
.Fn CRYPTO_mem_debug_push ,
and
.Fn CRYPTO_mem_debug_pop
are deprecated and are no-ops that always return 0.
.Pp
.Fn OPENSSL_strtoul
returns 1 on success and 0 in the event that an error has occurred.
Specifically, 0 is returned in the following events:
.Bl -bullet
.It
If the underlying call to strtoul returned a non zero errno value
.It
If the translation did not consume the entire input string, and the
passed endptr value was NULL
.It
If no characters were consumed in the translation
.El
.Pp
Note that a success condition does not imply that the expected
translation has been performed.
For instance calling
.Bd -literal
    OPENSSL_strtoul("0x12345", &endptr, 10, &num);
.Ed
.Pp
will result in a successful translation with num having the value 0, and
*endptr = 'x'.
Be sure to validate how much data was consumed when calling this
function.
.Sh HISTORY
.Fn OPENSSL_mem_debug_push ,
.Fn OPENSSL_mem_debug_pop ,
.Fn CRYPTO_mem_debug_push ,
.Fn CRYPTO_mem_debug_pop ,
.Fn CRYPTO_mem_leaks ,
.Fn CRYPTO_mem_leaks_fp ,
.Fn CRYPTO_mem_leaks_cb ,
.Fn CRYPTO_set_mem_debug ,
.Fn CRYPTO_mem_ctrl
were deprecated in OpenSSL 3.0. The memory-leak checking has been
deprecated in OpenSSL 3.0 in favor of clang's memory and leak sanitizer.
.Fn OPENSSL_aligned_alloc ,
.Fn CRYPTO_aligned_alloc ,
.Fn OPENSSL_strtoul
were added in OpenSSL 3.4.
